  "findings": [
    "Both windowed lanes were presented at the first attempt of each of their five slots: 10 of 10 runs accepted, none rejected, in the first live execution of the turn's path with the helper (the baseline's path had been exercised before the commit, in the exploratory execution).",
    "The engine could draw the window in every sampled frame: 0 of 25,469 in the baseline's five runs and 0 of 68,379 in the turn's, and 0 of 60 and 0 of 421 in the two captures runs.",
    "In every run the window opened drawable after the minimum 12 frames, with the floating level read back (alwaysOnTop true); window_move_to_foreground left the window focused in 8 of the 10 runs, and in the others the lack of focus did not keep it from drawing.",
    "Nobody was at the Mac during either lane, so the counts show that the helper opens a window the engine can draw and that the record is written and verified, not that it keeps the window drawing against interference."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Without the user present, this execution does not show that the helper beats interference (another window, a Space switch, a full-screen application): no moment of it put anything over the lane's window.",
    "A locked or asleep display is not covered: caffeinate -d only keeps an awake display awake, and the helper makes the window one the system has no reason to hide, not one a display shows.",
    "The captures run waits for frame_post_draw with no bound of its own: a window that cannot draw would hold it until the process timeout of the script.",
    "The counts are of the engine's flag (DisplayServer.window_can_draw) read at the start of each process frame, not of what the display showed.",
    "One machine, one display, one vsync mode; local only, the windowed lanes never run in hosted CI."
  ]
}
